-- A second batch of original English and Logical Reasoning MCQs, written for Markhor Prep against the PMDC MDCAT 2025 curriculum
-- (English learning outcomes 1.1 to 3.4, Logical Reasoning themes 5.1 to 5.6) and the NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmarks.
-- English ENG-0296 to ENG-0473 (178 questions), Logical Reasoning LR-0137 to LR-0216 (80 questions),
-- following on from migration 0021. Not taken from any book. Each question's source names the learning outcome it tests. Not in the free set.
--
-- Banks: English goes in MDCAT, AKU, and NUMS (NUMS leaves out the purely literary items: figures of speech, irony, satire, tone).
-- Logical Reasoning is only on the national MDCAT, so it gets no bank_questions rows.
-- INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice; if the site owner's admin account doesn't exist (a fresh local database)
-- nothing is inserted. Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0296', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "benign" most nearly means:
"The biopsy showed that the lump was benign, so the patient needed no further treatment."', '["cancerous", "painful", "not harmful", "growing quickly"]', 2, 'A benign growth is not harmful; the sentence says no further treatment was needed, which fits.', '{"0": "Cancerous is the opposite (malignant); it would need treatment.", "1": "Pain is not what ''benign'' describes.", "3": "Rapid growth would be a warning sign, not a reason to stop treatment."}'),
  ('ENG-0297', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "acute" most nearly means:
"He was rushed to the emergency room with acute pain in his abdomen that had started an hour earlier."', '["long-lasting and mild", "imaginary", "slowly improving", "sudden and severe"]', 3, 'In medicine ''acute'' means coming on suddenly and severely; the pain began only an hour earlier.', '{"0": "That describes ''chronic'', the opposite of acute.", "1": "Nothing suggests the pain is not real.", "2": "The rush to hospital shows it was getting worse, not better."}'),
  ('ENG-0298', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "chronic" most nearly means:
"Her chronic cough had troubled her every winter for more than ten years."', '["lasting a long time", "sudden", "infectious", "mild"]', 0, '''Chronic'' means persisting for a long time; the cough had lasted over ten years.', '{"1": "Sudden is the meaning of ''acute''.", "2": "Nothing in the sentence says the cough spreads to others.", "3": "The cough ''troubled'' her, so mild is not the meaning."}'),
  ('ENG-0299', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "dormant" most nearly means:
"The virus can remain dormant in the body for years before causing symptoms again."', '["dead", "inactive but able to become active", "spreading rapidly", "harmless forever"]', 1, 'Dormant means alive but inactive for a time; the virus later causes symptoms ''again''.', '{"0": "A dead virus could not cause symptoms later.", "2": "Spreading rapidly is the opposite of staying quiet.", "3": "It does cause symptoms again, so it is not harmless forever."}'),
  ('ENG-0300', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "lethal" most nearly means:
"Even a small dose of the poison can be lethal if it is not treated within an hour."', '["mild", "tasteless", "deadly", "slow-acting"]', 2, 'Lethal means able to cause death.', '{"0": "Mild contradicts the warning about treatment within an hour.", "1": "Taste is not mentioned.", "3": "The one-hour limit suggests it acts fast, not slowly."}'),
  ('ENG-0301', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "contagious" most nearly means:
"Measles is so contagious that one infected child can spread it to most of an unvaccinated class."', '["easily cured", "very rare", "caused by poor diet", "easily passed from person to person"]', 3, 'Contagious means spreading by contact between people, as the example of one child infecting a class shows.', '{"0": "Being easily cured is not about spreading.", "1": "The sentence shows it spreads widely, not that it is rare.", "2": "Diet is not mentioned; the disease is passed between children."}'),
  ('ENG-0302', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "ample" most nearly means:
"There was ample time to finish the paper, so nobody had to rush the last questions."', '["more than enough", "too little", "exactly enough", "wasted"]', 0, 'Ample means plentiful; nobody had to rush, so there was more than enough time.', '{"1": "Too little time would have made students rush.", "2": "''Ample'' suggests a surplus, not an exact amount.", "3": "Nothing says the time was wasted."}'),
  ('ENG-0303', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "vigilant" most nearly means:
"Nurses in the intensive care unit must remain vigilant through the night, watching every monitor."', '["sleepy", "watchful and alert", "careless", "cheerful"]', 1, 'Vigilant means carefully watching for danger, as the nurses watch every monitor.', '{"0": "Sleepy is the opposite of what the sentence requires.", "2": "Careless is the opposite of vigilant.", "3": "Mood is not the point; watchfulness is."}'),
  ('ENG-0304', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "superfluous" most nearly means:
"The editor removed the superfluous words, and the report became half as long but just as clear."', '["difficult", "important", "unnecessary", "misspelled"]', 2, 'Superfluous means more than is needed; removing those words did not reduce clarity.', '{"0": "Difficult words would not simply be cut without loss.", "1": "Removing important words would make the report less clear.", "3": "The words were cut for being extra, not for spelling."}'),
  ('ENG-0305', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "augment" most nearly means:
"The hospital hired extra nurses to augment its staff during the dengue season."', '["replace", "reduce", "train", "increase"]', 3, 'To augment is to make larger; extra nurses increase the staff.', '{"0": "The existing staff stayed; nobody was replaced.", "1": "Hiring more people does not reduce staff.", "2": "Training is not what hiring does."}'),
  ('ENG-0306', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "tedious" most nearly means:
"Copying the same table fifty times by hand was a tedious task that left everyone bored."', '["long and boring", "exciting", "dangerous", "quick"]', 0, 'Tedious means tiresome because it is long and dull; the task left everyone bored.', '{"1": "Exciting contradicts ''left everyone bored''.", "2": "Nothing about the task is risky.", "3": "Copying a table fifty times is not quick."}'),
  ('ENG-0307', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "resilient" most nearly means:
"Children are often resilient and recover quickly from illnesses that would weaken an adult for weeks."', '["fragile", "able to recover quickly", "careless", "infectious"]', 1, 'Resilient means able to recover quickly from difficulty, which the sentence explains directly.', '{"0": "Fragile is the opposite.", "2": "Carelessness has nothing to do with recovery.", "3": "The sentence is about recovering, not spreading illness."}'),
  ('ENG-0308', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "ambivalent" most nearly means:
"She felt ambivalent about moving abroad: excited by the opportunity but sad to leave her family."', '["completely certain", "angry", "having mixed feelings", "uninterested"]', 2, 'Ambivalent means having two opposing feelings at once, as the excited-but-sad description shows.', '{"0": "She is torn, not certain.", "1": "Anger is not one of the feelings described.", "3": "She cares a great deal, so she is not uninterested."}'),
  ('ENG-0309', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "exacerbate" most nearly means:
"Scratching the rash will only exacerbate the itching and may cause an infection."', '["relieve", "hide", "explain", "make worse"]', 3, 'To exacerbate is to make a problem worse; scratching increases itching and risks infection.', '{"0": "Relieve is the opposite meaning.", "1": "Scratching does not hide the itch.", "2": "Nothing is being explained."}'),
  ('ENG-0310', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "feasible" most nearly means:
"With only two weeks left, finishing the whole syllabus twice is not feasible."', '["possible to do", "necessary", "enjoyable", "expensive"]', 0, 'Feasible means practical or possible; two weeks is too little time.', '{"1": "The sentence is about whether it can be done, not whether it must be.", "2": "Enjoyment is not the issue.", "3": "Cost is not mentioned."}'),
  ('ENG-0311', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to ADEQUATE.', '["excellent", "sufficient", "lacking", "excessive"]', 1, 'Adequate means enough for the purpose, which is what sufficient means.', '{"0": "Excellent means very good, which is more than adequate.", "2": "Lacking is the opposite.", "3": "Excessive means too much, not just enough."}'),
  ('ENG-0312', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to DETER.', '["encourage", "delay", "discourage", "decide"]', 2, 'To deter is to discourage someone from doing something, often through fear of the result.', '{"0": "Encourage is the opposite.", "1": "Delay means to make later, not to discourage.", "3": "Decide is unrelated."}'),
  ('ENG-0313', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to VIVID.', '["dull", "faint", "secret", "bright and clear"]', 3, 'A vivid colour or memory is bright, strong and clear.', '{"0": "Dull is the opposite.", "1": "Faint is the opposite.", "2": "Secret is unrelated."}'),
  ('ENG-0314', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to RELUCTANT.', '["unwilling", "eager", "angry", "unable"]', 0, 'Reluctant means unwilling or hesitant to do something.', '{"1": "Eager is the opposite.", "2": "Anger is a different feeling.", "3": "Unable is about ability, not willingness."}'),
  ('ENG-0315', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to FATIGUE.', '["fever", "tiredness", "hunger", "anxiety"]', 1, 'Fatigue means extreme tiredness.', '{"0": "Fever is a raised body temperature.", "2": "Hunger is the need for food.", "3": "Anxiety is worry."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0316', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to TRIVIAL.', '["serious", "difficult", "unimportant", "ancient"]', 2, 'Trivial means of little value or importance.', '{"0": "Serious is close to the opposite.", "1": "Difficult is about effort, not importance.", "3": "Ancient means very old."}'),
  ('ENG-0317', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to ARTIFICIAL.', '["false", "man-made", "expensive", "natural"]', 3, 'Artificial means made by people; its opposite is natural.', '{"0": "False is close in meaning, not opposite.", "1": "Man-made is a synonym.", "2": "Price is unrelated."}'),
  ('ENG-0318', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to EXPAND.', '["contract", "enlarge", "explain", "extend"]', 0, 'To expand is to become larger; to contract is to become smaller.', '{"1": "Enlarge is a synonym.", "2": "Explain is unrelated.", "3": "Extend is close in meaning."}'),
  ('ENG-0319', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to HUMBLE.', '["modest", "arrogant", "poor", "gentle"]', 1, 'Humble means modest about oneself; arrogant means having too high an opinion of oneself.', '{"0": "Modest is a synonym.", "2": "Humble can describe poor circumstances, but the opposite of humble character is arrogance.", "3": "Gentle is close in meaning."}'),
  ('ENG-0320', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to TEMPORARY.', '["brief", "short-term", "permanent", "recent"]', 2, 'Temporary means lasting a short time; permanent means lasting for ever.', '{"0": "Brief is a synonym.", "1": "Short-term is a synonym.", "3": "Recent is about when, not how long."}'),
  ('ENG-0321', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to BENEVOLENT.', '["kind", "wealthy", "famous", "cruel"]', 3, 'Benevolent means kind and wishing to do good; cruel is the opposite.', '{"0": "Kind is a synonym.", "1": "Wealth is unrelated.", "2": "Fame is unrelated."}'),
  ('ENG-0322', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to VAGUE.', '["precise", "unclear", "distant", "quiet"]', 0, 'Vague means not clearly expressed; precise means exact and clear.', '{"1": "Unclear is a synonym.", "2": "Distance is unrelated.", "3": "Quiet is about sound."}'),
  ('ENG-0323', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word that best completes the sentence:
The fire did not just damage the old building; it completely ______ it, leaving nothing but ashes.', '["harmed", "destroyed", "affected", "scratched"]', 1, '''Leaving nothing but ashes'' needs the strongest word, destroyed.', '{"0": "Harmed is too weak for total loss.", "2": "Affected is far too mild.", "3": "Scratched means only slight surface damage."}'),
  ('ENG-0324', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word that best completes the sentence:
The students were not merely pleased with their results; they were ______.', '["satisfied", "content", "overjoyed", "calm"]', 2, '''Not merely pleased'' calls for a stronger feeling than pleased: overjoyed.', '{"0": "Satisfied is about the same as pleased.", "1": "Content is milder than pleased.", "3": "Calm does not show greater happiness."}'),
  ('ENG-0325', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Homophones', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the correct word for the blank:
The heavy rain had a bad ______ on the wheat crop.', '["affect", "effort", "afect", "effect"]', 3, '''Effect'' is the noun meaning result; ''had a bad ___'' needs a noun.', '{"0": "''Affect'' is usually a verb meaning to influence.", "1": "''Effort'' means hard work, which does not fit.", "2": "''Afect'' is not a word."}'),
  ('ENG-0326', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Homophones', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the correct word for the blank:
Please be quiet; the patients in the ward are trying to ______.', '["rest", "wrest", "rust", "rested"]', 0, '''Rest'' means to relax or sleep.', '{"1": "''Wrest'' means to pull something away by force.", "2": "''Rust'' is the coating that forms on iron.", "3": "''Trying to'' must be followed by the base form, not ''rested''."}'),
  ('ENG-0327', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Homophones', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the correct word for the blank:
The surgeon needed a steady hand to ______ the wound.', '["sow", "sew", "so", "saw"]', 1, '''Sew'' means to stitch with a needle and thread.', '{"0": "''Sow'' means to plant seeds.", "2": "''So'' is a conjunction or adverb.", "3": "''Saw'' is a past tense of ''see'' or a cutting tool; it does not mean stitch."}'),
  ('ENG-0328', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Scanning a passage', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Antibiotics kill bacteria, but they have no effect on viruses, which cause most colds and flu. When people take antibiotics they do not need, or stop a course early, the bacteria that survive are the toughest ones. These survivors multiply and pass on their resistance, so the next infection may not respond to the usual drugs. Doctors therefore urge patients to take antibiotics only when prescribed and to finish the full course, even if they feel better after two days.
According to the passage, why are antibiotics not useful for most colds?', '["Colds clear up within two days", "Antibiotics are too expensive", "Most colds are caused by viruses, which antibiotics do not affect", "Cold bacteria are always resistant"]', 2, 'The passage says antibiotics ''have no effect on viruses, which cause most colds and flu''.', '{"0": "The ''two days'' refers to feeling better during a course, not to colds.", "1": "Cost is never mentioned.", "3": "The passage does not say cold bacteria are always resistant; it says colds are mostly viral."}'),
  ('ENG-0329', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Main idea', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Antibiotics kill bacteria, but they have no effect on viruses, which cause most colds and flu. When people take antibiotics they do not need, or stop a course early, the bacteria that survive are the toughest ones. These survivors multiply and pass on their resistance, so the next infection may not respond to the usual drugs. Doctors therefore urge patients to take antibiotics only when prescribed and to finish the full course, even if they feel better after two days.
What is the main idea of the passage?', '["Viruses are more dangerous than bacteria", "Doctors prescribe too many antibiotics", "Colds should be treated at home", "Misusing antibiotics helps bacteria become resistant to them"]', 3, 'Every sentence builds towards how unnecessary or incomplete use creates resistant bacteria.', '{"0": "The passage does not compare how dangerous viruses and bacteria are.", "1": "The passage blames patients'' misuse, not doctors'' prescribing.", "2": "Home treatment of colds is not discussed."}'),
  ('ENG-0330', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Inference from a passage', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Antibiotics kill bacteria, but they have no effect on viruses, which cause most colds and flu. When people take antibiotics they do not need, or stop a course early, the bacteria that survive are the toughest ones. These survivors multiply and pass on their resistance, so the next infection may not respond to the usual drugs. Doctors therefore urge patients to take antibiotics only when prescribed and to finish the full course, even if they feel better after two days.
Why do doctors ask patients to finish the course even if they feel better?', '["Stopping early can leave the toughest bacteria alive to multiply", "Feeling better is a sign of resistance", "The medicine stops working after two days", "Unused tablets cannot be thrown away"]', 0, 'The passage explains that stopping early means ''the bacteria that survive are the toughest ones'' and these multiply.', '{"1": "Feeling better is a sign of improvement, not resistance.", "2": "The passage does not say the medicine stops working.", "3": "Disposal of tablets is not mentioned."}'),
  ('ENG-0331', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Antibiotics kill bacteria, but they have no effect on viruses, which cause most colds and flu. When people take antibiotics they do not need, or stop a course early, the bacteria that survive are the toughest ones. These survivors multiply and pass on their resistance, so the next infection may not respond to the usual drugs. Doctors therefore urge patients to take antibiotics only when prescribed and to finish the full course, even if they feel better after two days.
In the passage, the word "urge" most nearly means:', '["forbid", "strongly advise", "force", "forget"]', 1, 'Doctors ''urge'' patients, i.e. they strongly encourage them to follow the advice.', '{"0": "Forbid means to not allow, which is the opposite.", "2": "Doctors advise; they cannot force patients.", "3": "Forget makes no sense in the sentence."}'),
  ('ENG-0332', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Scanning a passage', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
In 1854, cholera killed hundreds of people in one part of London within a few days. Most doctors believed the disease spread through bad air. John Snow, a local doctor, marked each death on a map and noticed that the deaths clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street. He persuaded the authorities to remove the pump''s handle, and the number of new cases soon fell. His careful use of evidence helped show that cholera is spread through contaminated water.
What did John Snow notice when he marked the deaths on a map?', '["The deaths were spread evenly across London", "Most deaths happened near hospitals", "The deaths were grouped around one water pump", "The deaths followed the direction of the wind"]', 2, 'The passage says ''the deaths clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street''.', '{"0": "The passage says they clustered, not that they were spread evenly.", "1": "Hospitals are not mentioned.", "3": "Wind would support the bad-air idea, which the map did not show."}'),
  ('ENG-0333', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Inference from a passage', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
In 1854, cholera killed hundreds of people in one part of London within a few days. Most doctors believed the disease spread through bad air. John Snow, a local doctor, marked each death on a map and noticed that the deaths clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street. He persuaded the authorities to remove the pump''s handle, and the number of new cases soon fell. His careful use of evidence helped show that cholera is spread through contaminated water.
Which statement is best supported by the passage?', '["Most doctors in 1854 agreed with John Snow", "Cholera is spread through bad air", "Removing the pump handle had no effect", "Evidence from observation can overturn a widely held belief"]', 3, 'Most doctors believed in bad air, but Snow''s map evidence showed the water was to blame.', '{"0": "The passage says most doctors believed in bad air, not in Snow''s idea.", "1": "This is the belief the passage shows to be wrong.", "2": "The passage says new cases ''soon fell''."}'),
  ('ENG-0334', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
In 1854, cholera killed hundreds of people in one part of London within a few days. Most doctors believed the disease spread through bad air. John Snow, a local doctor, marked each death on a map and noticed that the deaths clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street. He persuaded the authorities to remove the pump''s handle, and the number of new cases soon fell. His careful use of evidence helped show that cholera is spread through contaminated water.
In the passage, the word "clustered" most nearly means:', '["gathered closely together", "disappeared", "spread out", "increased slowly"]', 0, 'Clustered means grouped close together, here around one pump.', '{"1": "The deaths did not disappear.", "2": "Spread out is the opposite.", "3": "The word is about position, not the speed of increase."}'),
  ('ENG-0335', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Tone and irony', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
In 1854, cholera killed hundreds of people in one part of London within a few days. Most doctors believed the disease spread through bad air. John Snow, a local doctor, marked each death on a map and noticed that the deaths clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street. He persuaded the authorities to remove the pump''s handle, and the number of new cases soon fell. His careful use of evidence helped show that cholera is spread through contaminated water.
The writer''s attitude towards John Snow is best described as:', '["critical", "admiring", "amused", "indifferent"]', 1, 'Phrases like ''his careful use of evidence'' show the writer approves of and respects Snow.', '{"0": "Nothing in the passage criticises Snow.", "2": "The tone is serious, not humorous.", "3": "The writer clearly values his work, so is not indifferent."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

